# DESCRIPTION

Processes share data by sharing memory, not by encoding it.
Memory is a VMO, a run of pages named by a handle (see vmo(2)); a task that holds the handle maps the VMO into its address space with as(2), and two tasks that map one VMO see the same pages.
A handle is sent through a channel, or beside a ring's entry in one of its handle slots (see rings(7)), so the data never passes through a message: what is sent is the right to map it.

A handle's rights say what its holder may do with the VMO, and a handle given away may have fewer rights than the giver's, never more (see handle_dup(2)): a VMO to read but not to write is a duplicate without `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`.
Mapping needs `VX_RIGHT_MAP` and `VX_RIGHT_READ`, and a writable mapping `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`.
handles(7) describes rights.

The same pieces carry a file's pages and a device's buffers.
fsd(4) gives a mapped file as one VMO of its page cache, shared by every process that maps the file; a 9Px read or write can take its data from a VMO rather than the message; and a disk's driver gives its device the client's own buffers by DMA.
Bulk data moves by sharing pages, and is copied only where a copy is cheaper than a mapping, or where the receiver must have bytes that cannot change under it.

@node=vmos title="Memory objects" keys=vmo,anonymous,physical,pager,clone,rights,commit

A VMO is one of three kinds.
An anonymous VMO, vmo_create(2), has all its pages allocated and zeroed when it is made: memory is committed, not overcommitted, so a task learns it has run out from a failed call, never from a fault later.
A physical VMO is a device's memory, which only a driver is given.
A pager-backed VMO starts with no pages, and a trusted task supplies them as they are touched (see {files|#files}).
A VMO is a whole number of 4096-byte pages, at most 256 MiB, and lives until its last handle and its last mapping are gone.

`vmo_rw` copies bytes between a VMO and the caller's memory without mapping it, as a server does with a VMO it does not trust enough to map.
`vmo_clone` makes a new anonymous VMO holding a copy of a range of another, at once.

@node=mapping title="Mapping" keys=as_map,address,offset,fixed,fork,futex

`as_map` maps a range of a VMO into a task, at an address the kernel picks or, given one, at that address if its pages are free.
A mapping is readable always, and writable or executable but never both; a JIT maps one VMO twice.
The mapping holds the VMO, so its handle may be closed once it is mapped.
A task holds at most 102 mappings.

Since each side picks its own address, a structure in shared memory links its parts by offsets from the start of the VMO, which mean the same in every task that maps it.
Plain pointers need both sides to map the VMO at one base, which `as_map` can be asked for, but nothing yet reserves that range for the receiver, and the kernel places its own mappings one after another from a fixed base.

A lock in shared memory is a futex (see futex(2)), keyed by the word's physical address, so the word one VMO holds is one futex in every task that maps it.

`fork` copies a task's anonymous mappings into new VMOs of the child's, so an anonymous VMO the parent shares with another task is the child's own copy; a pager-backed mapping, a mapped file, is shared with the child, and a ring's memory is not mapped in the child at all.
POSIX's `mmap` of a file with `MAP_SHARED` is so shared across `fork`; `MAP_SHARED` of anonymous memory is refused.

@node=publish title="Handing over a structure" keys=publish,commit,version,offset,table,trust,plugin,validate

The kernel's design (`docs/01-kernel-ipc.md` §6.6) gives one discipline for handing another process a whole structure, a syntax tree or a symbol table, to read in place, and today's calls hold most of it.

- **Build, then send.** The writer builds the structure in a VMO of its own and then sends a handle to it. The send is the commit point: a writer that dies while building has sent nothing, so no reader sees half a structure. A new version is a new VMO, never the old one written again.
- **Link by offsets.** A link is an offset from the VMO's start, which the reader checks against the VMO's size before it follows it, once for each link.
- **Link between VMOs through a table.** A structure in several VMOs, sent together in one message, names each by its position among the message's handles, and a link is a pair: a slot in a table at the head of the VMO, and an offset. The reader maps each handle once, and checks a slot against the table's length as it checks an offset. A position in a message, not a global name, so a link carries no authority the message did not.
- **Trust follows the direction.** A trusted writer sends its reader a handle without `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`, and the reader reads in place. A reader that does not trust its writer treats the VMO as a ring's memory is treated (see rings(7)): it reads each value it depends on once, into memory of its own, and checks it there, since until a VMO can be sealed nothing stops a writer that kept its handle from changing the bytes under it.

So a host and a plugin can be separate processes, a plugin that crashes taking nothing of the host with it, and neither serialises what it shares.
The cost is that a structure built to be shared is laid out for it, in one VMO with offsets, rather than spread over the writer's heap.

@node=files title="Files as memory" keys=tmap,mmap,pager,page,cache,fault,dirty,writeback,dref,treadref,twriteref

A file is mapped through 9Px's `Tmap` (see map(5)), which only fsd(4) answers, as the system's pager.
A pager is a trusted task that supplies the pages of the VMOs it makes (see pager(2)): only a service whose manifest says `pager` holds the authority, so a server that is not trusted cannot stall another process's page fault.
`fsd` answers every `Tmap` of a file, from any connection and any user, with the same VMO, the file's page cache, so every process that maps the file maps the same pages, and every `Tread` and `Twrite` of it sees them too.

A thread that touches a page not yet there faults, and the kernel asks the pager on its port; the thread waits, up to the pager's deadline, for the page to be supplied, and takes a `VX_EXCEPTION_PAGER_TIMEOUT` exception, POSIX's `SIGBUS`, if it is not.
A page is mapped read-only until it is written; that write marks it dirty, and `fsd` asks the kernel for the dirty pages and writes them back every 5 seconds while files are mapped, and before each commit.
Only the pager resizes the VMO, so no process can shrink a file under the others by its mapping.
The kernel's own copies to and from user memory do not fault pages in, so a program touches a mapped file's pages before it hands them to a system call.

The musl back end sends `Tmap` for POSIX's `mmap` of a file that is `MAP_SHARED`, or `MAP_PRIVATE` and not writable; a writable private mapping is read into a VMO of its own.

`Treadref` and `Twriteref` (see dref(5)) are a read and a write whose data is in a VMO of the client's, sent beside the request, not in the message: one message moves any amount, whatever the msize, and `fsd` copies once, between the volume and the VMO.
`fsd` does not map a client's VMO, since it may be `fsd`'s own page cache, which it cannot fault on itself; it copies with `vmo_rw`.

@node=open-files title="Open files shared" keys=share,join,token,offset,fork,exec,append

An open file's offset is state, not memory, and it is shared through the file's server.
With the `posix` extension a server keeps an open file for each fid it opens: its offset and whether it appends (see share(5)).
`Tshare` gives a token for an open file, 16 random bytes good for a number of joins, and `Tjoin` on any connection to the same server makes a fid on the same open file; the token is the authority, and permissions are not checked again.
A token's holds run out 10 seconds after the file's last share or its last close, so one no child took cannot be used later.

The musl back end shares each open file so before a `fork` and writes the tokens into the child's spawn record, and the child joins them on its own connections: parent and child then move one offset, as POSIX says they do.
posix(7) describes the rest of a POSIX program's descriptors.

@node=devices title="Devices" keys=dma,iommu,domain,pin,driver,arena

A device reaches memory by DMA only through a DmaDomain (see dma(2)), which devmgr(8) makes for each PCI function and keeps.
`dma_map` gives the device a range of an anonymous VMO, and the device addresses of its pages, and holds the pages for the device until it is unmapped; where an IOMMU covers the function, the device can reach nothing else.
A pager's VMO cannot be mapped for a device, since its pages come and go.

The block drivers, drv-virtio-blk(3) and drv-nvme(3), keep the memory of each session's ring and map its client arena for their device, so a disk reads into and writes from the client's buffers with no copy in the driver (see rings(7)).

# EXAMPLES

From `tests/user/dreftest.c`, run on `fsd` with its `home` branch on `/tmp`: a file written from a 200 KiB VMO in one `Twriteref`, more than ten of the ring's 16 KiB messages, then read back with one `Treadref` into another VMO at an offset (lines rewrapped):

```c
CHECK(vx_vmo_create(SIZE, 0, &out) == VX_OK &&
      vx_vmo_create(SIZE + 4096, 0, &in) == VX_OK);
CHECK(vx_vmo_rw(out, VX_VMO_WRITE, 0, data, SIZE) == VX_OK);
CHECK(vx_ns_create(&ns, VX_STR("/tmp/dref"), 0644, P9_ORDWR, &f) == VX_OK);
CHECK(p9c_writeref(f.c, f.fid, 0, out, 0, SIZE, &done) == VX_OK &&
      done == SIZE);
CHECK(p9c_readref(f.c, f.fid, 0, in, 4096, SIZE, &done) == VX_OK &&
      done == SIZE);
```

From `tests/posix/maptest.c`, a POSIX program: a file mapped shared, written through the mapping, and read back through a second attach of the same branch after a commit, as another process would see it:

```c
char *p = mmap(nullptr, SIZE, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, MAP_SHARED, fd, 0);
memset(p, 'D', SIZE);
CHECK(msync(p, SIZE, MS_SYNC) == 0);
CHECK(ctl("sync"));
int other = open("/s/m.bin", O_RDONLY);
CHECK(other >= 0 && read_all(other, back) && all(back, 'D'));
```

